## Context map

<!--
The edges between the contexts above — which context's model flows into
which, and on what terms. One line per edge, in the shape

  - **<Context A> → <Context B>**: <relationship> — <role>; <what crosses>.

and EVERY edge is declared from both sides, each under the context that is
speaking: the same relationship word on both lines, and only the role
differs (upstream on one, downstream on the other). Two declarations of one
edge that name different relationships are the disagreement itself, made
visible before it becomes a bug. An edge declared from one side only is half
a decision — a reader can tell, because the other context's block does not
mention it.

The relationships are Evans's set. Name exactly one per edge:

  - upstream / downstream — not a relationship but the two ROLES on any
    edge: the upstream's model flows to the downstream, and the downstream
    has no veto.
  - conformist — the downstream adopts the upstream's model as-is, no
    translation.
  - anti-corruption layer — the downstream translates the upstream's model
    into its own at the edge, and nothing upstream leaks past it.
  - published language — a documented interchange model both sides
    translate to and from, owned by neither.
  - shared kernel — a small, named subset of the model owned jointly and
    changed only by agreement of both; the roles are then "co-owner".
  - separate ways — no integration at all; duplication accepted on purpose.

The design brief writes this section; the grilling loop keeps it current.
-->

### <Context name>

- **<Context name> → <Second context name>**: conformist — upstream;
  publishes `<Event>`, spelled exactly as the term above.

### <Second context name>

- **<Second context name> → <Context name>**: conformist — downstream;
  consumes `<Event>` as-is and keeps no model of its own for it.

